{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018MaVK6DoQmpUrGCEYA7pbC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: grep-rulebook\n3\tdescription: Look up Magic: The Gathering rules in /workspace/RULEBOOK.txt using grep. Use whenever you're unsure how a keyword, timing rule, replacement effect, state-based action, or other mechanic actually works — before guessing in a card_impl.py or engine/ change.\n4\t---\n5\t\n6\t`RULEBOOK.txt` (workspace root) is the Magic: The Gathering Comprehensive Rules — the authoritative source for any rules question (keyword behavior, timing, replacement vs trigger ordering, state-based actions, etc.). **Whenever you are unsure how a mechanic works, check the rulebook before guessing.** A wrong card implementation costs you a card; an incorrect-by-design helper in `engine/` can fail many cards at once.\n7\t\n8\t## Read with grep, not cat / Read\n9\t\n10\t`RULEBOOK.txt` is very large. Reading the whole file blows your context budget and returns mostly noise. **Always grep.** Use `Read` only to expand a specific line range you've located with grep.\n11\t\n12\t## File structure (so you know what to grep for)\n13\t\n14\tThe file has two halves:\n15\t\n16\t1. **Numbered rules** (lines ~13–3,860) — hierarchical: section (e.g. `700.`), rule (e.g. `702.2`), subrule (e.g. `702.2a`). Common sections you'll hit a lot:\n17\t   - `100.`–`199.` — game concepts (mana, damage, counters, priority)\n18\t   - `300.`–`399.` — card types (Creature, Instant, Sorcery, Enchantment, Land, Artifact, Planeswalker)\n19\t   - `500.`–`599.` — turn structure (phases, steps, untap, upkeep, combat)\n20\t   - `600.`–`699.` — spells and abilities (casting, resolving, targeting, replacement effects)\n21\t   - `701.` — keyword actions (counter, destroy, exile, sacrifice, regenerate, …)\n22\t   - `702.` — keyword abilities (flying, trample, lifelink, deathtouch, ward, …)\n23\t   - `704.` — state-based actions\n24\t2. **Glossary** (lines ~3,861 onward) — alphabetical term definitions. Often the fastest path for \"what does X mean?\"\n25\t\n26\t## Grep recipes\n27\t\n28\t```bash\n29\t# 1. Look up a keyword ability by name\n30\t#    702. is the keyword-ability section. Most keywords get one numbered subrule.\n31\tgrep -n \"^702\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"deathtouch\"\n32\t# → 3877:702.2. Deathtouch\n33\t# Then expand:\n34\tgrep -n -A 20 \"^702\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -30\n35\t\n36\t# 2. Pull a specific rule + its subrules with context\n37\tgrep -n -A 30 \"^704\\.5\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # state-based-action enumeration\n38\tgrep -n -A 50 \"^601\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # casting-a-spell steps\n39\t\n40\t# 3. Glossary lookup (case-insensitive, anchored on a line that starts with the term)\n41\t#    Note: only proper keyword abilities have glossary entries. \"Ability words\"\n42\t#    (converge, landfall, magecraft, …) intentionally don't — they're flavor\n43\t#    tags with no rules text. If ^term returns nothing, fall through to (4).\n44\tgrep -n -i \"^deathtouch\" RULEBOOK.txt\n45\tgrep -n -i \"^lifelink\" RULEBOOK.txt\n46\t\n47\t# 4. Find every mention of a mechanic\n48\tgrep -ni \"replacement effect\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n49\tgrep -ni \"ward\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n50\t\n51\t# 5. Find which numbered rule discusses a topic, then drill in\n52\tgrep -n -E \"^[0-9]{3}\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"combat\"\n53\t# → e.g. 506. Combat Phase\n54\tgrep -n -A 80 \"^506\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\tOnce you've narrowed to a line range, switch to `Read` with `offset` + `limit` to pull just that block (cheaper than re-grepping).\n58\t\n59\t## Best practices\n60\t\n61\t- **Anchor with `^`** on rule-number lookups (`^702\\.`, `^704\\.5\\.`). Without the anchor you'll match every cross-reference to those rules elsewhere in the doc.\n62\t- **Escape the dot** in rule numbers (`702\\.2\\.`) — grep treats `.` as any char. With BRE this matches anyway, but the explicit escape is unambiguous.\n63\t- **Start narrow, then widen.** Try the specific rule number first (`702.19` for Trample). If you don't know the number, search the glossary (`^trample`). If neither hits, search the body with `-i`.\n64\t- **Use `-A` / `-B` for context.** Rules are paragraph-shaped; `-A 20` typically captures a rule plus its first few subrules without overflowing context.\n65\t- **Cap the output with `| head -N`** on broad searches — `grep -i \"creature\"` returns hundreds of hits.\n66\t- **Cross-reference, don't reinvent.** Card rules text often paraphrases the rulebook; when in doubt, the numbered rule wins over your reading of the card.\n67\t\n68\t## When NOT to consult the rulebook\n69\t\n70\t- The behavior is already encoded in `engine/` and a working FDN reference card uses it the same way — trust the FDN example.\n71\t- The card's spec (`card_spec.json`) plus the engine source make the behavior obvious. Don't waste a grep on basic things like \"does Flying restrict who can block this\" (the FDN flying cards already show you).\n72\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4fef623c-942b-4e62-9572-a5757d885aba","uuid":"a8c5b173-64e2-4894-b861-05f3a3820fe2","timestamp":"2026-06-11T01:46:32.373Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/skills/grep-rulebook/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: grep-rulebook\ndescription: Look up Magic: The Gathering rules in /workspace/RULEBOOK.txt using grep. Use whenever you're unsure how a keyword, timing rule, replacement effect, state-based action, or other mechanic actually works — before guessing in a card_impl.py or engine/ change.\n---\n\n`RULEBOOK.txt` (workspace root) is the Magic: The Gathering Comprehensive Rules — the authoritative source for any rules question (keyword behavior, timing, replacement vs trigger ordering, state-based actions, etc.). **Whenever you are unsure how a mechanic works, check the rulebook before guessing.** A wrong card implementation costs you a card; an incorrect-by-design helper in `engine/` can fail many cards at once.\n\n## Read with grep, not cat / Read\n\n`RULEBOOK.txt` is very large. Reading the whole file blows your context budget and returns mostly noise. **Always grep.** Use `Read` only to expand a specific line range you've located with grep.\n\n## File structure (so you know what to grep for)\n\nThe file has two halves:\n\n1. **Numbered rules** (lines ~13–3,860) — hierarchical: section (e.g. `700.`), rule (e.g. `702.2`), subrule (e.g. `702.2a`). Common sections you'll hit a lot:\n   - `100.`–`199.` — game concepts (mana, damage, counters, priority)\n   - `300.`–`399.` — card types (Creature, Instant, Sorcery, Enchantment, Land, Artifact, Planeswalker)\n   - `500.`–`599.` — turn structure (phases, steps, untap, upkeep, combat)\n   - `600.`–`699.` — spells and abilities (casting, resolving, targeting, replacement effects)\n   - `701.` — keyword actions (counter, destroy, exile, sacrifice, regenerate, …)\n   - `702.` — keyword abilities (flying, trample, lifelink, deathtouch, ward, …)\n   - `704.` — state-based actions\n2. **Glossary** (lines ~3,861 onward) — alphabetical term definitions. Often the fastest path for \"what does X mean?\"\n\n## Grep recipes\n\n```bash\n# 1. Look up a keyword ability by name\n#    702. is the keyword-ability section. Most keywords get one numbered subrule.\ngrep -n \"^702\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"deathtouch\"\n# → 3877:702.2. Deathtouch\n# Then expand:\ngrep -n -A 20 \"^702\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -30\n\n# 2. Pull a specific rule + its subrules with context\ngrep -n -A 30 \"^704\\.5\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # state-based-action enumeration\ngrep -n -A 50 \"^601\\.2\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt        # casting-a-spell steps\n\n# 3. Glossary lookup (case-insensitive, anchored on a line that starts with the term)\n#    Note: only proper keyword abilities have glossary entries. \"Ability words\"\n#    (converge, landfall, magecraft, …) intentionally don't — they're flavor\n#    tags with no rules text. If ^term returns nothing, fall through to (4).\ngrep -n -i \"^deathtouch\" RULEBOOK.txt\ngrep -n -i \"^lifelink\" RULEBOOK.txt\n\n# 4. Find every mention of a mechanic\ngrep -ni \"replacement effect\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\ngrep -ni \"ward\" RULEBOOK.txt | head -20\n\n# 5. Find which numbered rule discusses a topic, then drill in\ngrep -n -E \"^[0-9]{3}\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt | grep -i \"combat\"\n# → e.g. 506. Combat Phase\ngrep -n -A 80 \"^506\\.\" RULEBOOK.txt\n```\n\nOnce you've narrowed to a line range, switch to `Read` with `offset` + `limit` to pull just that block (cheaper than re-grepping).\n\n## Best practices\n\n- **Anchor with `^`** on rule-number lookups (`^702\\.`, `^704\\.5\\.`). Without the anchor you'll match every cross-reference to those rules elsewhere in the doc.\n- **Escape the dot** in rule numbers (`702\\.2\\.`) — grep treats `.` as any char. With BRE this matches anyway, but the explicit escape is unambiguous.\n- **Start narrow, then widen.** Try the specific rule number first (`702.19` for Trample). If you don't know the number, search the glossary (`^trample`). If neither hits, search the body with `-i`.\n- **Use `-A` / `-B` for context.** Rules are paragraph-shaped; `-A 20` typically captures a rule plus its first few subrules without overflowing context.\n- **Cap the output with `| head -N`** on broad searches — `grep -i \"creature\"` returns hundreds of hits.\n- **Cross-reference, don't reinvent.** Card rules text often paraphrases the rulebook; when in doubt, the numbered rule wins over your reading of the card.\n\n## When NOT to consult the rulebook\n\n- The behavior is already encoded in `engine/` and a working FDN reference card uses it the same way — trust the FDN example.\n- The card's spec (`card_spec.json`) plus the engine source make the behavior obvious. Don't waste a grep on basic things like \"does Flying restrict who can block this\" (the FDN flying cards already show you).\n","numLines":72,"startLine":1,"totalLines":72}}}
